{"id":12781486818407,"type":"artist","name":"John Fahey","artist":null,"year":1939,"weight":47,"info":{"tags":["folk","blues","guitar","instrumental","experimental"],"country":"US","begin":"1939-02-28","end":"2001-02-22","kind":"Person","similar":["Jack Rose","Robbie Basho","Glenn Jones","Leo Kottke","Elizabeth Cotten","Sandy Bull","Sir Richard Bishop","Daniel Bachman","Mississippi John Hurt","Six Organs of Admittance"],"disambiguation":"American composer and guitarist","img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Scan_J_Fahey_1_Paris_1984_web.jpg?width=500","bio":"John Fahey (28 de febrero de 1939 - 22 de febrero de 2001) fue uno de los guitarristas estadounidenses que ha marcado pauta en el uso de la guitarra acústica como único instrumento. Su estilo ha sido muy influyente, que en ciertas entrevistas el mismo lo definió como \"Primitivo Americano\", término tomado de la pintura y que hace referencia a su arte autodidacta. Fahey se inclina generalmente a un estilo que recoge el folk, ragtime, country y blues"},"works":[{"id":76170616236499,"type":"album","name":"Vol. II: Death Chants, Breakdowns, & Military Waltzes","artist":"John Fahey","year":1964,"weight":8,"yt":"thc8dwkzSBk","src":"musthear","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":87310548737747,"name":"RYM Top — Folk — Contemporary Folk","rank":119,"scaruffi":null},{"id":94905110718967,"name":"RYM Top — Folk","rank":124,"scaruffi":null},{"id":121946165801189,"name":"RYM Top — Regional Music — Northern American Music","rank":42,"scaruffi":null},{"id":173816365235609,"name":"RYM Top — Folk — American Primitivism","rank":6,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":4}},{"id":78733535032476,"type":"album","name":"The Transfiguration of Blind Joe Death","artist":"John Fahey","year":1965,"weight":5,"yt":"2lUEYMwYk4I","src":"charts,musthear","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":32093215757431,"name":"Pitchfork: The 200 Best Albums of the 1960s","rank":74,"scaruffi":null},{"id":4389278164509,"name":