,"end":1973,"desc":"John Fahey introduce el flujo de conciencia en el folk, lo convierte en música clásica y le hace cruzar la frontera entre Oriente y Occidente: el «raga occidental» de sus largas meditaciones a la guitarra evoca paisajes y fantasmas de pioneros. Robbie Basho lleva la guitarra solista de Fahey al misticismo oriental antes de que existieran el new age, la world music o la fusión.","color":"#a3e635","family":"Folk y cantautores","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#american-primitive"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":82098797668078,"artist":"John Fahey","artist_id":12781486818407,"title":"Great San Bernardino Party","year":1966,"ess":1,"yt":"wMjBI8HtKKs","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Folk progresivo (American primitive)","scene_id":125346883647083,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#american-primitive","chapters":["Sección 2.7"],"pages":[[1,28,null]]},{"id":93712229646389,"artist":"John Fahey","artist_id":12781486818407,"title":"Requia","year":1967,"ess":1,"yt":"QWz5BXOFpQc","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Folk progresivo (American primitive)","scene_id":125346883647083,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#american-primitive","chapters":["Sección 2.7"],"pages":[[1,28,null]]},{"id":237832692825393,"artist":"John Fahey","artist_id":12781486818407,"title":"Voice Of The Turtle","year":1968,"ess":1,"yt":"9cMQTo_pVIE","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Folk progresivo (American primitive)","scene_id":125346883647083,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#american-primitive","chapters":["Sección 2.7"],"pages":[[1,28,null]]},{"id":161034051299229,"artist":"John Fahey","artist_id":12781486818407,"title":"America","year":1971,"ess":1,"yt":"hiXPGeZpGi8","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Folk progresivo (American primitive)","scene_id":125346883647083,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#american-primitive","chapters":["Sección 2.7"],"pages":[[1,28,null]]},{"id":100260746087356,"artist":"John Fahey","artist_id":12781486818407,"title":"Fare Forward Voyagers","year":1973,"ess":1,"yt":"KrMuPhqRzZg","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Folk progresivo (American primitive)","scene_id":125346883647083,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#american-primitive","chapters":["Sección 2.7"],"pages":[[1,28,null]]},{"id":7481265597486,"artist":"John Fahey","artist_id":12781486818407,"title":"A Raga Called Pat Part 3 & 4","year":null,"ess":0,"yt":"jOx3NyG_1e8","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Folk progresivo (American primitive)","scene_id":125346883647083,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#american-primitive","chapters":["Sección 2.7"],"pages":[[1,28,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":21,"text":"…While the music of protest singers was not expected to be innovative, other folk musicians focused just on that: innovation. Davy Graham in Britain, John Fahey on the West Coast, and Sandy Bull (12) on the East Coast played and composed pieces that fused folk, blues, jazz and Indian raga, while Joseph…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":21,"text":"…his status as one of the overlooked geniuses of the era. While he would compose his best works several years later, it was around this time that John Fahey, following the intuition of Pete Seeger's Goofing Off Suite (1955), invented \"American primitivism\", a new way of exploiting the folk and country…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":28,"text":"…stance to acid-rock's visionary format on Electric Music For Mind And Body (1967) and I Feel Like I'm Fixin To Die (1967). Progressive folk John Fahey (114) is the man who introduced the stream of consciousness into folk music, and turned folk music into classical music, and then made it cross the boundaries of western and eastern music. The spiritual father of the \"american primitive guitar\", Fahey turned the guitar solo into a metaphysical exercise. Great San Bernardino Party (1966) and Requia (1967) introduced his surreal world of tragic and…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":29,"text":"explorers, the anxiety of the adventurers, the hopes of the pioneers are joined together, but Fahey shuns the epic mode and prefers a form of domestic impressionism, which is tender and warm. His art is about the collective myths of mankind. His…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":29,"text":"…the \"Ulysseses\" who traveled (walked, rode, sailed) towards the unknown. The mysterious Daniel Robinson, better known as Robbie Basho (23), took up John Fahey's solo-guitar music, wed it to eastern mysticism (way before new-age music was invented), mixed in elements of middle-eastern, Indian, Latin and…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":80,"text":"…Me In Your Arms (1975) was an easy-going laid-back attitude. Progressive bluegrass 1971-75 The \"progressive bluegrass\" movement (a brainchild of John Fahey) that blossomed during the first half of the 1970s represented an artistic alternative to country-rock. While country-rock was focusing on the conventions of the country ballad, a number of country-related musicians explored more adventurous formats. John Fahey's \"primitivism\" infected a naive soul such as Leo Kottke (3), a virtuoso guitarist whose 6 & 12 String Guitar (1972), Greenhouse (1972) and My Feet…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":81,"text":"…and Tony Rice's Acoustics (1979) were the first albums to implement the master's vision. The others would follow in the 1980s. In the tradition of John Fahey's avantgarde folk music, Eugene Chadbourne (2) was a free improviser whose roots were in rural white music but whose technique borrowed from jazz…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":201,"text":"…the melody. Ackerman's main reason to found his label had been, actually, the scarce availability of records in the solo acoustic vein of John Fahey, and Fahey would remain another important prototype for all solo new-age musicians. Other 201"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":230,"text":"…the leading groups of instrumental neo-prog came out of Boston: Cul De Sac (3). The lengthy tracks on Ecim (1992) bridged German rock of the 1970s, John Fahey's transcendental folk, Terry Riley's minimalism and Pink Floyd's psychedelic ragas. Their most innovative work, China Gate (1996), increased the…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":236,"text":"…Ringing In The Sky (1999), led to the tour de force of Ayahuasca (2001), whose \"ragas\" defined a post-psychedelic and post-ambient music bridging John Fahey, Grateful Dead, Ravi Shankar and LaMonte Young. Connecticut's Primordial Undermind (2) evolved from the garage-rock of Yet More Wonders Of The…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":241,"text":"…walked an even more arduous path: the impressionistic vignettes of Scenes From The South Island (1995) harked back to the transcendental spirit of John Fahey, to the divine introspection of Peter Green, and to the dreamy psalms of David Crosby; while an obscure, symbol-drenched metaphysics and an…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":241,"text":"…solo project White Winged Moth, that devoted albums such as I Can See Inside Your House (1996) to instrumental vignettes situated halfway between John Fahey and Derek Bailey, and finally with the spiritual, ambient, psychedelic and ethnic collections under his own name, such as Moth Park (1998) and All…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":243,"text":"…between slow, tortured dirges that flowed towards controlled cacophony, somber, colloquial meditations, majestic, symphonic, twang-drenched odes, John Fahey-ian East/West fusion, jazz-rock, raga, etc. The Amazing California Health And Happiness Road Show (2000) includes their tour de force, Burn. While…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":260,"text":"…relied both on lengthy hypnotic jamming and on simple, manageable form. Martsch's relentless guitar ruminations created the noise-rock equivalent of John Fahey's \"primitive guitar\": introspection, meditation on the meaning of life, contemplation of the universe, and worship of the absolute. Keep It Like A…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":294,"text":"…which yielded the ambient blend of jazz, rock and dub of When In Vanitas (1994). O'Rourke's experimental fury subsided with the tributes to John Fahey's \"primitive guitar music\" of Happy Days (1997) and especially Bad Timing (1997). And then, suddenly, he reinvented himself in the tradition of…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":312,"text":"…Varese premieres his Poeme Electronique in a special pavilion designed by architect Le Corbusier, where the music reacts with the environment ？ John Fahey invents \"American primitivism\" ？ Bobby Freeman's Do You Wanna Dance begins the \"dance craze\" ？ The Columbia-Princeton studio is established in New…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":316,"text":"…Astral Weeks invents abstract, free-form folk-rock by fusing soul, jazz, folk and psychedelia ？ Tim Buckley's Happy Sad fuses folk and free-jazz ？ John Fahey's Voice Of The Turtle fuses instrumental folk, jazz and raga ？ The Pentangle and the Fairport Convention debut, the leading groups of British…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":318,"text":"…Byrd Brothers ？ Malcom McLaren opens a boutique in London that becomes a center for the non-conformist youth ？ Other significant albums of the year: John Fahey's America, Captain Beefheart's Mirror Man, Can's Tago Mago, Kevin Ayers's Shooting At The Moon, Robbie Basho's Song of The Stallion, Joni Mitchell's…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":319,"text":"…introduces the SH-1000, Japan's first synthesizer ？ Other significant albums of the year: Popol Vuh's Hosianna Mantra, Gong's Radio Gnome Invisible, John Fahey's Fare Forward Voyagers, New York Dolls's I, Magma's Mekanik Destruktiw Kommandoh, Faust's IV, Klaus Schulze's Cyborg ？ The Rocky Horror Picture…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":330,"text":"…Heel Jack's Disappeared ？ Sales for the record industry slip 5% (first decline in ages) ？ Napster is found guilty of breaching copyright law ？ John Fahey dies ？ Sandy Bull dies ？ Fred Neil dies 2001 ？ Joey Ramone dies ？ Florian Fricke of Popol Vuh dies ？ \"Puncture\" ceases publications ？ 62 albums…"}],"podcast":[],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":76170616236499,"type":"album","name":"Vol. II: Death Chants, Breakdowns, & Military Waltzes","list_id":94905110718967,"list":"RYM Top — Folk","src":"musthear","rank":124,"year":1964,"note":null,"yt":"thc8dwkz